systemd: stop tmpfiles requiring STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT it only uses as a hint

Attempt 8 left five failed units, and systemd-tmpfiles-clean.service is the one
that only appears after the timer first fires, which is why it is not in the
original four:

  systemd-tmpfiles-clean.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=73

Running it by hand shows the same sentence 0006 and 0013 already made familiar,
once per directory it was asked to clean:

  statx(/var/tmp/systemd-private-...-dhcpcd.service-1vQLB7/tmp) failed: Protocol driver not attached
  statx() does not support 'STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT' attribute (running on an old kernel?)
  statx(/var/tmp) failed: Protocol driver not attached

"Protocol driver not attached" is EUNATCH, which src/basic/stat-util.c returns
when a *mandatory* attribute is missing from stx_attributes_mask.  Both statx()
calls in the cleanup path pass STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT in the mandatory_attributes
argument, so on a kernel that predates it (5.8) xstatx_full() gives up before it
fills in the mode, uid and timestamps that are the actual reason for the call.
Every entry then fails, and the unit exits 73.

The attribute is not load-bearing at either site.  It answers one question --
"is this entry the root of a different mount, and therefore not mine to clean?"
-- and the answer is used to skip.  So ask for it optionally, use it when the
kernel reports it, and otherwise ask is_mount_point_at(), which patch 0013 has
already taught to answer on this kernel by comparing mount ids from
/proc/self/fdinfo.  Both the STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT test and that fallback are
about mount roots specifically, so the meaning is preserved rather than
approximated.

The FLAGS_SET(stx_attributes_mask, ...) test is what distinguishes "the kernel
told us this is not a mount root" from "the kernel cannot tell us".  Without it
an absent attribute reads as a cleared one, and stx_attributes would silently
answer "not a mount point" for everything -- which is the failure mode where
tmpfiles cleans across a mount boundary rather than refusing to clean at all.
A kernel that reports the attribute takes exactly the path it takes today.

Worth noting what produced the noise in the first place: nearly every directory
in that output is a /var/tmp/systemd-private-*-dhcpcd.service-* left behind by a
unit that has been failing and restarting since boot (patch 0015).  The cleaner
that was supposed to collect them is this one, on a rootfs already at 98%.  The
two fixes are independent, but they compound.
--- a/src/tmpfiles/tmpfiles.c
+++ b/src/tmpfiles/tmpfiles.c
@@ -615,12 +615,20 @@
                         /* xstatx_flags= */ 0,
                         STATX_MODE|STATX_INO,
                         STATX_ATIME|STATX_MTIME,
-                        STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT,
+                        /* mandatory_attributes= */ 0,
                         &sx);
         if (r < 0)
                 return log_error_errno(r, "statx(%s) failed: %m", path);
 
-        *ret_mountpoint = FLAGS_SET(sx.stx_attributes, STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT);
+        if (FLAGS_SET(sx.stx_attributes_mask, STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT))
+                *ret_mountpoint = FLAGS_SET(sx.stx_attributes, STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT);
+        else {
+                r = is_mount_point_at(dirfd(d), /* path= */ NULL, /* flags= */ 0);
+                if (r < 0)
+                        return log_error_errno(r, "Failed to determine whether %s is a mount point: %m", path);
+
+                *ret_mountpoint = r;
+        }
         *ret = TAKE_PTR(d);
         *ret_sx = sx;
         return 1;
@@ -716,7 +724,7 @@
                                 /* xstatx_flags= */ 0,
                                 STATX_TYPE|STATX_MODE|STATX_UID,
                                 STATX_ATIME|STATX_MTIME|STATX_CTIME|STATX_BTIME,
-                                STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT,
+                                /* mandatory_attributes= */ 0,
                                 &sx);
                 if (r == -ENOENT)
                         continue;
@@ -727,7 +735,21 @@
                         continue;
                 }
 
-                if (FLAGS_SET(sx.stx_attributes, STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT)) {
+                bool is_mount_root;
+                if (FLAGS_SET(sx.stx_attributes_mask, STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT))
+                        is_mount_root = FLAGS_SET(sx.stx_attributes, STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT);
+                else {
+                        r = is_mount_point_at(dirfd(d), de->d_name, /* flags= */ 0);
+                        if (r < 0) {
+                                log_full_errno(r == -EACCES ? LOG_DEBUG : LOG_ERR, r,
+                                               "Failed to determine whether %s/%s is a mount point, ignoring: %m", p, de->d_name);
+                                continue;
+                        }
+
+                        is_mount_root = r;
+                }
+
+                if (is_mount_root) {
                         log_debug("Ignoring \"%s/%s\": different mount points.", p, de->d_name);
                         continue;
                 }

